13:28:40 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Actually, this is a common misconception, so let's clarify what reading research actually shows versus what gets simplified in early education. **What you may have learned in school** was likely about *decoding*—the process of sounding out or recognizing words to learn to read (especially relevant for phonics-based instruction). That's about *skill acquisition*, not about how skilled adult readers process text in real-time. **What eye-tracking research actually shows** about fluent adult reading is different: - Using eye-trackers, researchers (like Keith Rayner, a pioneer in this field) found that readers *don't* fixate on every word. Eyes move in jumps called **saccades**, and short, predictable function words (the, a, of, in) are skipped entirely somewhere between **50-70% of the time**. - Even when a word *is* fixated on, average fixation times are very brief (~200-250ms), which is enough for basic recognition but not necessarily robust error-checking against duplicates. - There's some parafoveal (peripheral) processing—your eyes gather partial information about upcoming words before fixating on them—but this is a rough preview, not full processing. **So why did you almost certainly not immediately spot "the the" in your own message?** It's a great real-world demo of exactly this skipping phenomenon. It's not that your brain literally saw two instances of "the" and consciously decided to ignore one—it's that the second "the" likely wasn't fully fixated on or processed as a discrete unit at all, because your brain predicted the sentence structure and moved on. This is a well-documented effect called the **"missing word effect"** or related to something researchers call the **repeated word blindness** phenomenon—and it's *precisely because* full word-by-word processing (in the sense of consciously registering each word) *isn't* what happens during fluent reading. So the "brain processes every word" idea isn't wrong in a loose sense (you do extract *meaning* from nearly the whole text), but it's misleading if taken to mean *conscious, individuated attention on every single word*—that's not how skilled reading works.
